# The two voices of a 1, in one transcript. A refusal and a runtime error end
# the same way -- stderr, status 1 -- because the only question a shell asks
# is whether there is a report it can use, and both answer no. What a reader
# gets is the difference: the program's own sentence, with no position
# anywhere in it, against a diagnostic with a kind, a line and a caret.
#
# The status is why this is a `.cli` case and not a `.vine` one. A refusal in
# process is `tests/cases/fail.vine`, and the runner records it as that case's
# failure; the thing it cannot record is the number the shell is handed, which
# is half of what `fail` exists to do.
#
# The last two are the one place `fail` reads as a function and is not one.
# `fail("no rows to report")` is the parentheses grouping an expression, so
# its chunk below has to be the previous one word for word -- the identity is
# the golden, and a reader can see it without running anything. A second
# argument is then a comma inside a group that was never a call, and the
# message says so truthfully without saying *`fail` is not a function*, which
# is the cost of the grouping and is written down in **Refusing**.
tests/fixtures/refuses.vine
-e 'fail "no rows to report"'
-e 'fail("no rows to report")'
-e 'fail("no rows", 1)'
-e 'fail'
tests/fixtures/broken.vine
